# Project agent instructions

## Requirements

Follow `REQUIREMENTS.md` and `TODO.md`, when present, for the document format and every task rule. Do not assume a fixed style.

Create `REQUIREMENTS.md` or `TODO.md` only if it does not exist, and write to it only while it is empty. Once either has content, never modify it: the only allowed change is checking or unchecking `TODO.md` items, and only for items that are complete and meet what the files establish.

## Build and inspection

Never run `compile.sh` or any other compilation command unless the user explicitly asks.

To inspect or validate the compiled PDF, use only this command, which always skips the cover page and keeps the file name (it carries the author) out of the output:

```bash
pdftotext -f 2 -layout build/*.pdf -
```

Never open the PDF or read its metadata by any other means. If no PDF exists, ask the user to compile it.

## Private data

Never read, write or commit anything under `data/`.

## Skills

Treat one agent conversation as one session. Load each required skill at most once per session and reuse its instructions. Reload it only in a new conversation, after context compaction removed its instructions, when its `SKILL.md` changed, or when the user asks.

- `file-naming`: load before any project task and follow it for every file or directory you create or rename. A path change is incomplete until all references are updated.
- `latex-writing`: load before reading, creating, editing, reviewing or formatting any LaTeX document (`*.tex`, `*.sty`, `*.cls`) and follow it.
- `research-helper`: load only during research sessions; outside them, do not use it. The `research/` directory is the local source base, indexed in `research/_index.md`: consult it instead of the internet and change it only in research sessions.

## Git

Before editing the template, check that the project root is inside a Git repository; if not, run `git init`. Right after, tell the user in the chat to define their credentials with these commands, using their own data, and wait for their confirmation:

```bash
git config user.name "<name>"
git config user.email "<email>"
```

Once the user confirms, check that it works with `git var GIT_AUTHOR_IDENT` and report the result in the chat.

Keep commits simple and minimal, split by similar or shared responsibility. Never commit build artifacts, generated PDFs, logs or backups unless the user explicitly asks.

## References

Store references as BibTeX entries in `main.bib` with lowercase ASCII `kebab-case` keys and explicit fields for every known bibliographic component. Add only references validated by the agent and confirmed by the user. Entries already in `main.bib` count as validated and are not re-verified.
